  "slug": "learning-biology-small-c2ee",
  "summary": "Nine preprints pair matched-budget ADMET benchmarks with large bioactivity and folding-model encoders, testing which representations hold up under strict…",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is controlled evaluation of ML models for small-molecule and peptide property prediction. Benchmarks compare active-learning strategies across eight ADMET endpoints, GNNs against frozen ChemBERTa/MolCLR and fingerprints on six TDC endpoints under scaffold splits, and endpoint-specific tuning of gradient-boosted models (Ark-ADMET). PeptSol adds a protected-peptide solubility benchmark with chemistry-aware splits. Several papers probe limits: MET kinase deep mutational scanning models are largely ligand-invariant, and immunogenicity labels constrain antibody design, with a format lookup outperforming sequence predictors. New modeling directions include pocket-conditioned property range prediction (RangeFinder), PSICHIC+ trained on ~82M protein-ligand records, and FoldAbS repurposing frozen folding models as antibody encoders. Together, they emphasize matched protocols over uniform gains from any one model family.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-05"
}
